  nodeTextLayers:
  - title: What is the SNARE Complex?
    basic: This is a SNARE complex. It's made up of multiple peptide chains embedded
      in a membrane.
    intermediate: 
    advanced: "A SNARE complex is formed when v-SNAREs (vesicle-associated SNAREs)
      and t-SNAREs (target membrane-associated SNAREs) form a stable complex. \nIn
      insulin vesicles, these SNARE proteins are syntaxin, SNAP-25/23, and VAMP2.
      \nThe core of the SNARE complex is a bundle of 4 helices from different SNARE
      proteins."
  - title: Why is the SNARE Complex important?
    basic: The SNARE complex enables site-directed membrane fusion in the cell, which
      is required for normal cellular functions.
    intermediate: 
    advanced: The SNARE complex cooperates with membrane-trafficing proteins known
      as synaptotagmin to sense chemical signals, allowing the cell to regulate membrane
      fusion.
  nodeSpriteLayer: []
